Arsalan: So Müller (2016)  suggests that the danger of democracy is anti-pluralism: leaders who claim that they are the only ones representing the people and that the opponent is illegitimate. For this theory, Ghana comes out to be an outlier. Now, digging deeper into the score of political rights, which is 35/40. Ghana has only 2 major political parties. While smaller parties tried to join elections, they were deregistered for failing requirements, and high filing fees make it harder for smaller parties to join. Ghana has been an interesting country to do research on, and maybe digging more will change my view further.
Afnan: Certainly, you're right on that. Before we close, there is one limitation that I have analysed while making this comparison between the four countries. We have all talked only about institutional design and political competition as the main drivers of these outcomes. We need to keep in mind other factors such as colonial legacy, population size, geography, and others that shaped these trajectories to be how they are right now. If we compare Singapore and Uruguay, they differ in size as well as historical background. This divergent path may reflect why it's not only institutional choices alone. Furthermore, comparing only four countries is too bold a basis for a global claim. Like Bellin's bounded generalization, our comparison is case-specific and illustrative, not a universal prediction (Bellin, 2012).
